---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import DropZone from '../../components/DropZone.astro';
import MovieFaq from '../../components/MovieFaq.astro';

const path = '/for/portraits/';
const title = 'Cinematic Portrait Photo Filter | Cinematic Photo Editor';
const description =
	'Five cinematic presets chosen specifically for portraits: skin-tone-safe color grades that add mood without turning faces an unnatural color. Apply one to your photo instantly.';

const structuredData = {
	'@context': 'https://schema.org',
	'@type': 'CollectionPage',
	name: title,
	description,
	url: `https://cinematicphoto.com${path}`,
};

const faqs = [
	{
		q: 'Which cinematic presets are safest for skin tones?',
		a: "The five featured on this page, Shawshank, The Prestige, Top Gun: Maverick, Life of Pi, and Spirited Away, all use a warm-leaning or balanced color split that keeps skin looking like skin rather than shifting it toward an unnatural hue. They were picked specifically because their overlays don't dump a single flat color across the whole frame.",
	},
	{
		q: 'Why do some cinematic filters make skin look sickly or unnatural on faces?',
		a: "Presets built around a strong, flat color tint, like a heavy green or magenta wash, apply that color to everything in the frame equally, including skin. On landscapes or architecture that reads as stylish; on a face it often reads as an odd, unhealthy color cast. Presets with a shadow/highlight color split rather than a flat tint are far more forgiving on faces.",
	},
	{
		q: 'Can I use a dark, moody preset for a dramatic portrait instead of these five?',
		a: "Yes, but it needs the right lighting: a dark and moody grade works on portraits lit by one strong, directional light source, a window, a lamp, backlight, where shadows already have shape. On flat, evenly-lit portraits it tends to just darken the frame. See the dark and moody photo filter guide for that specific look.",
	},
	{
		q: 'Which preset works best for wedding or family portraits?',
		a: "Shawshank's warm, restrained 35mm grade is the most versatile for groups and mixed skin tones, it's gentle enough not to overpower a range of faces in one frame. Top Gun: Maverick's glossy sky-blue and amber pairs well with outdoor, golden-hour wedding shots specifically.",
	},
	{
		q: 'How do I avoid oversaturating skin tones when applying a cinematic preset?',
		a: "Start with one of the five presets on this page rather than a saturation-heavy grade, then use the grain, vignette, and glow sliders in the editor to add mood without pushing color intensity further. If a preset still feels too strong, applying it to a slightly brighter original photo softens the effect.",
	},
	{
		q: 'Do group photos need a different preset than single portraits?',
		a: "Not a different preset, but pay closer attention to even lighting across all subjects, an uneven-light group photo will show a heavier grade's shadow crush unevenly across faces. The gentler grades here, Shawshank and The Prestige especially, are more forgiving when lighting varies across a group.",
	},
];
---

<Layout {title} {description} {path} structuredData={structuredData}>
	<Navbar variant="landing" />
	<main>
		<Breadcrumbs
			items={[
				{ label: 'Home', href: '/' },
				{ label: 'For' },
				{ label: 'Portraits' },
			]}
		/>

		<section class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-3xl flex-col items-center px-4 pb-4 text-center sm:px-6">
			<span class="mb-4 font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-mute">USE CASE GUIDE</span>
			<h1 class="font-sans text-4xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink sm:text-5xl">
				Cinematic portrait photo filter
			</h1>
			<p class="mt-5 max-w-xl text-base leading-7 text-body">
				Not every movie grade is safe for faces. These five are chosen specifically for portraits:
				enough mood to feel cinematic, without turning skin an unnatural color.
			</p>
		</section>

		<DropZone
			presetIds={['shawshank', 'the-prestige', 'top-gun-maverick', 'life-of-pi', 'spirited-away']}
			showHeading={false}
			showFooterLinks={false}
		/>

		<section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 py-14 sm:px-6">
			<span class="mb-4 block font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-mute">WHAT TO LOOK FOR</span>
			<h2 class="font-sans text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				What makes a preset portrait-safe
			</h2>
			<div class="mt-6 flex flex-col gap-5 text-base leading-7 text-body">
				<p>
					Most cinematic grades split color between shadows and highlights rather than tinting
					the whole frame one flat hue, and that split matters most on faces. A warm-leaning
					highlight tone keeps skin looking like skin; a preset built around a single strong color
					wash, applied evenly across the entire image, can shift skin toward green, magenta, or
					another unnatural cast that reads fine on a landscape but poorly on a person.
				</p>
				<p>
					Contrast and vignette also matter more on portraits than on wide shots: too aggressive
					and shadow detail on a face crushes into featureless black. The five presets above lean
					warm or balanced and keep contrast moderate, which is why they're featured here instead
					of the tool's harder, more desaturated grades.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 pb-14 sm:px-6">
			<span class="mb-4 block font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-mute">USE WITH CAUTION ON FACES</span>
			<h2 class="font-sans text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				Presets to think twice about for portraits
			</h2>
			<div class="mt-6 flex flex-col gap-5 text-base leading-7 text-body">
				<p>
					The <a href="/presets/the-matrix/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">Matrix preset</a>'s
					flat green tint applies uniformly across the frame by design, striking on an urban scene,
					but it will read as an unusual color cast on skin. Very high-vignette, crushed-shadow
					grades like the ones on the <a href="/looks/dark-and-moody/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">dark and moody photo filter</a> page
					can work for dramatic, hard-lit portraits specifically, but tend to flatten detail on
					softly, evenly-lit faces.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 pb-14 sm:px-6">
			<span class="mb-4 block font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-mute">GO DEEPER</span>
			<h2 class="font-sans text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				More ways to shape the look
			</h2>
			<div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
				<a href="/looks/vintage-35mm-film/" class="rounded-md border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 transition-colors hover:border-hairline-strong">
					<span class="block font-sans text-sm font-medium text-ink">Vintage 35mm film look</span>
					<span class="mt-1 block font-mono text-xs text-mute">Gentle, warm, and forgiving on skin tones</span>
				</a>
				<a href="/looks/dark-and-moody/" class="rounded-md border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 transition-colors hover:border-hairline-strong">
					<span class="block font-sans text-sm font-medium text-ink">Dark &amp; moody cinematic filter</span>
					<span class="mt-1 block font-mono text-xs text-mute">For dramatic, hard-lit portraits specifically</span>
				</a>
				<a href="/guides/how-to-make-photos-look-cinematic/" class="rounded-md border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 transition-colors hover:border-hairline-strong">
					<span class="block font-sans text-sm font-medium text-ink">How to make photos look cinematic</span>
					<span class="mt-1 block font-mono text-xs text-mute">The complete step-by-step guide</span>
				</a>
				<a href="/presets/" class="rounded-md border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 transition-colors hover:border-hairline-strong">
					<span class="block font-sans text-sm font-medium text-ink">Browse all 50 presets</span>
					<span class="mt-1 block font-mono text-xs text-mute">Every color grade, organized by genre</span>
				</a>
			</div>
		</section>

		<MovieFaq eyebrow="FAQ" heading="Common questions about cinematic portrait filters" faqs={faqs} />
	</main>
	<Footer />
</Layout>

<script>
	import '../../lib/landing';
</script>
